    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#court-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Court Size</a>
          <a href="#kitchen" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Kitchen</a>
          <a href="#service-courts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Service Courts and Lines</a>
          <a href="#net" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Net Height and Posts</a>
          <a href="#playing-area" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Space Around the Court</a>
          <a href="#tennis" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Converting a Tennis Court</a>
          <a href="#home" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Driveway and Garage Courts</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A pickleball court is small, which is a big part of why the sport spread so fast. The playing area is 20 feet wide and 44 feet long, the same size as a doubles badminton court, and it is identical for singles and doubles. Inside that rectangle sit a 7-foot non-volley zone on each side of the net, known as the kitchen, and two service courts on each side. Getting those lines and the net height right matters more than most people expect, because a few inches either way changes how the game plays. This guide gives every measurement, then shows how to lay out a court on a tennis court, a driveway or a garage floor.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="court-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Court Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            A regulation pickleball court is 20 feet wide by 44 feet long, or about 6.10 by 13.41 metres. USA Pickleball specifies that court measurements are made to the outside of the perimeter lines, so the lines themselves are part of the court. A ball that lands on a sideline or baseline is in, which is why measuring to the outside edge matters when you mark a court.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same court is used for singles and doubles. Unlike tennis, there are no separate tramlines for doubles, which makes pickleball courts simpler to mark and easier to share between formats. The net divides the court into two 22-foot halves, one for each side.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Measurement</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feet</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Metres (approx.)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Court width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.10 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Court length</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13.41 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Each half (net to baseline)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.71 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Non-volley zone depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.13 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Service court depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.57 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Service court width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.05 m</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Line width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 cm</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those dimensions are fixed for regulation play, but recreational courts are often squeezed into whatever space is available. A slightly short court still works for casual games, but it changes the balance between the kitchen and the baseline, and it shortens the time players have to react. If you can fit a full 20 by 44 foot court, do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equipment is the other half of a good game. Once your court is marked, our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> will help you choose a paddle, and the <a href="/best-pickleball-balls/">best pickleball balls</a> guide covers the right ball for indoor or outdoor courts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kitchen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Kitchen (Non-Volley Zone)
          </h2>

          <p>
            The non-volley zone, almost always called the kitchen, is the area that extends 7 feet from the net on each side and runs the full 20-foot width of the court. Players may not volley, meaning hit the ball out of the air, while standing in the kitchen or touching its line. They can step in to play a ball that has bounced.
          </p>

          <p>
            The kitchen line is part of the kitchen, so touching it during a volley is a fault. That makes the line's placement critical: measure 7 feet from the net to the outside edge of the kitchen line, so the 2-inch line sits inside the zone. A kitchen marked a few inches too deep or too shallow changes how the soft game near the net plays.
          </p>

          <p>
            The kitchen is what gives pickleball its character. It stops players from camping at the net and smashing every ball, and it rewards soft shots, called dinks, that drop into the opponent's kitchen. Much of the strategy at higher levels happens around this line, which is why accurate marking matters even on home courts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The kitchen also changes footwear needs. Players move side to side constantly along the kitchen line, which puts lateral stress on shoes. Running shoes are not built for that movement; court shoes are. Our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">best pickleball shoes</a> explains why lateral support and grip matter on this small court.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="service-courts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Service Courts and Lines
          </h2>

          <p>
            Behind each kitchen, the remaining 15 feet of each half is split down the middle by a centre line into two service courts, each 10 feet wide and 15 feet deep. Serves must land in the diagonally opposite service court, beyond the kitchen line, so the kitchen line also acts as the front edge of the service area.
          </p>

          <p>
            All lines are 2 inches wide and should contrast clearly with the court surface. The court is bounded by two sidelines and two baselines, with the kitchen lines parallel to the net and the centre lines running from each kitchen line back to the baseline. The centre line does not extend into the kitchen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Marking order makes the job easier. Start with the outer rectangle, check that it is square by measuring both diagonals, which should be equal at about 48.3 feet for a 20 by 44 foot court, then add the kitchen lines and finally the centre lines. Squaring the court first prevents a slightly skewed rectangle, which is the most common home-marking mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            For permanent courts, paint lines with a sports-court line paint designed for your surface. For temporary or shared courts, removable court tape or chalk works well. Some community courts use a different colour for pickleball lines when they overlap tennis lines, which helps players see the right boundaries.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Measure to the outside of the lines, and check the diagonals.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The 20 by 44 foot court includes its lines, and the kitchen line is part of the kitchen. Equal diagonals of about 48.3 feet confirm the rectangle is square before you add the inside lines.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="net" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Net Height and Posts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The net is 36 inches high at the sidelines and 34 inches high at the centre. That 2-inch sag is part of the specification, not a sign of a slack net, and it makes the middle of the net slightly lower, which affects how players aim dinks and drives. Many nets include a centre strap to hold the 34-inch height.
          </p>

          <p>
            Net posts sit just outside the sidelines, so the net spans the full 20-foot width of the court with a little extra on each side. Portable nets are typically around 22 feet wide for this reason. Check the height at both posts and in the centre with a tape measure before play, because portable nets often sag or lean over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pickleball nets are lower than tennis nets, which are 42 inches at the posts and 36 inches at the centre. That difference is why playing pickleball on a tennis court with the tennis net unchanged does not work well: the net is too high at the centre and much too high at the posts. Most converted courts use a separate pickleball net or lower the tennis net to the pickleball height.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable nets are the easiest option for home courts and shared spaces. They set up in minutes and store in a bag, but they vary in stability. Look for a sturdy frame, a centre support to hold the 34-inch height, and a net wide enough to cover the full court width. Weighting the feet helps on windy days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="playing-area" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Space Around the Court
          </h2>

          <p>
            The court itself is only part of the space you need. Players run behind the baseline and outside the sidelines, so a court needs room around it to be safe and playable. The USA Pickleball rulebook lists a minimum total playing surface of 30 by 60 feet, and recommends 34 by 64 feet for new construction and tournament play.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those figures leave roughly 5 to 7 feet on each side and 8 to 10 feet behind each baseline. That margin lets players chase wide balls and retreat for lobs without running into a fence or a wall. On a cramped home court, the lack of run-off space is often more limiting than the court size itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are building or marking multiple courts side by side, allow space between them as well, so players on neighbouring courts do not collide. Fencing between courts helps keep balls on their own court, which is a common frustration at busy community sites.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoors, check ceiling height and obstacles such as basketball hoops, lights and wall fixtures. Lobs need vertical space, and a low ceiling changes the game. Gyms marked for badminton often work well because the court size matches, though the net height and kitchen lines are different and need adding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tennis" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Converting a Tennis Court
          </h2>

          <p>
            A tennis court is far larger than a pickleball court, at 36 by 78 feet for doubles, so it can host pickleball in several ways. The simplest is to mark one pickleball court inside the tennis court and use a portable pickleball net, or lower the tennis net to pickleball height. That gives one court quickly without permanent changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many facilities go further and fit multiple pickleball courts on the footprint of a single tennis court, using the extra space outside the tennis lines. A common arrangement fits up to four courts on one fenced tennis-court area with portable nets, though the exact number depends on the run-off space the facility wants to keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mark pickleball lines on a tennis court, use a different colour from the tennis lines so players can tell them apart. Temporary tape or chalk lets you try a layout before committing to paint. Check with the court owner first, because many public courts have rules about permanent lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surface of an outdoor tennis court suits outdoor pickleball well, so use an outdoor ball, as our guide to <a href="/indoor-vs-outdoor-pickleball-balls/">indoor vs outdoor pickleball balls</a> explains. Outdoor balls are harder and have more holes, so they cope with the textured surface and any wind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Driveway and Garage Courts
          </h2>

          <p>
            A full 20 by 44 foot court, plus run-off space, needs roughly the area of a large two-car driveway or more. Many driveways are narrower or shorter than that, so home courts are often slightly shortened. For casual play and practice, a reduced court still works, but keep the 7-foot kitchen and the net height correct, because those affect the game most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mark a driveway court with court tape or chalk, measure the outer rectangle first and check the diagonals for square, then add the kitchen and centre lines. A portable net around 22 feet wide covers the full width. On a sloped driveway, the slope affects bounces, so a flat section is worth more than a few extra feet of length.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage floors are smooth concrete, which plays more like an indoor surface. An indoor ball can work well in a closed garage, while an outdoor ball suits an open driveway with wind. Watch for oil stains and dust, which make the surface slippery, and clean the floor before playing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about neighbours and safety. Pickleball is loud, and the pop of paddle on ball carries. Keep the court away from bedroom windows, check local rules, and make sure there is nothing breakable behind the baselines. With the right space, a home court is one of the easiest ways to practise. If you are just starting, our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-beginners/">beginner paddle picks</a> and <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">how to choose a pickleball paddle</a> are good next reads, along with our main <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">pickleball paddle guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring to the Inside of the Lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Court dimensions are measured to the outside of the perimeter lines, so the lines are part of the court. Measuring to the inside edge makes the court about 4 inches too big in each direction. Measure to the outside edge so the 20 by 44 foot rectangle includes its lines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Getting the Kitchen Depth Wrong</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The kitchen extends 7 feet from the net, and its line is part of the zone. Marking it too deep or too shallow changes the soft game near the net. Measure 7 feet to the outside edge of the kitchen line, on both sides, and check the measurement at each sideline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Tennis Net at Tennis Height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tennis nets are 42 inches at the posts and 36 inches at the centre, well above pickleball's 36 and 34 inches. Playing over a tennis net at full height makes dinks and drives far harder. Use a portable pickleball net or lower the tennis net to pickleball height.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Diagonal Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rectangle that looks square can be skewed by several inches, which throws off every line inside it. Measure both diagonals of the outer rectangle before adding inner lines; they should be equal, at about 48.3 feet for a 20 by 44 foot court.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving No Run-Off Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Players need room behind the baselines and beside the sidelines to chase balls safely. A court marked right up to a wall or fence is cramped and risky. Aim for the rulebook's minimum total playing surface of 30 by 60 feet where possible, or as close as your space allows.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big is a pickleball court?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pickleball court is 20 feet wide by 44 feet long, about 6.10 by 13.41 metres, measured to the outside of the lines. The same court is used for singles and doubles. USA Pickleball lists a minimum total playing surface of 30 by 60 feet, including space around the court.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big is the kitchen in pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The kitchen, or non-volley zone, extends 7 feet from the net on each side and runs the full 20-foot width of the court. The kitchen line is part of the zone, so a player touching it while volleying commits a fault. Measure to the outside edge of the line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high is a pickleball net?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pickleball net is 36 inches high at the sidelines and 34 inches high at the centre. The slight sag in the middle is part of the specification. Tennis nets are higher, at 42 inches at the posts and 36 inches at the centre, so they need lowering for pickleball.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are singles and doubles pickleball courts the same size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Unlike tennis, pickleball uses the same 20 by 44 foot court for singles and doubles, with no separate doubles lines. That makes courts simpler to mark and easier to share between formats. Only the serving and positioning rules differ between singles and doubles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pickleball courts fit on a tennis court?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One pickleball court fits easily inside a tennis court's lines. Many facilities fit up to four pickleball courts on the footprint of a single fenced tennis-court area, using portable nets and the space outside the tennis lines. The exact number depends on the run-off space kept around each court.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide are pickleball court lines?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pickleball court lines are 2 inches wide, and they are included in the court measurements. Lines should contrast clearly with the surface. On shared tennis courts, a different colour for pickleball lines helps players tell the two sets of boundaries apart.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a pickleball court on my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you have enough flat space. A full court plus run-off needs a large area, so many driveway courts are slightly shortened for casual play. Keep the 7-foot kitchen and correct net height, use tape or chalk for lines, and choose an outdoor ball for an open driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

